From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 15:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(android): inline-3D on by default, opt out with
 --disable-inline-3d

The Android preview shipped its headline feature switched off. inline-3D was
reachable only via `--enable-inline-3d`, which on Android arrives through
/data/local/tmp/chrome-command-line — a file that needs adb. Our own test
tablets have carried it since bring-up, so every internal check passed while
every external tester saw a browser that renders the whole web normally and
shows nothing where the 3D should be.

Two colleagues hit this independently within days of each other. Neither could
have diagnosed it: the runtime reports a clean PASS (it is idle, nothing ever
connects to it), the demos all weave correctly (they run in-process and never
touch the browser's path), and the failure looks exactly like a broken browser
rather than a feature that was never turned on. One of them went through a
runtime reinstall, a browser reinstall and a vendor-services update chasing it.

So: on Android, default the switch ON in ChromeMainDelegate::BasicStartupComplete
unless --disable-inline-3d is given. This is the earliest callback and runs
before the FeatureList is created, so everything downstream — including the
SurfaceControl suppression added in 0096, which reads the same switch a few
lines below — sees a command line indistinguishable from one where the user
passed the flag by hand. No call site changes; `HasSwitch("enable-inline-3d")`
keeps working everywhere, and the existing manual flag file remains a no-op
addition rather than a conflict.

Windows and macOS are untouched: they gate inline-3D on their own paths and
this block is IS_ANDROID-only.

Opt-out rather than opt-in is the right default for a device whose entire
reason to exist is the glasses-free display. A preview whose feature is off by
default is indistinguishable from a broken one — which is precisely how both
testers reported it.
---
 chrome/app/chrome_main_delegate.cc | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+)

diff --git a/chrome/app/chrome_main_delegate.cc b/chrome/app/chrome_main_delegate.cc
--- a/chrome/app/chrome_main_delegate.cc
+++ b/chrome/app/chrome_main_delegate.cc
@@ -1143,6 +1143,28 @@ std::optional<int> ChromeMainDelegate::BasicStartupComplete() {
   }
 #endif  // BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN)
 
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_ANDROID)
+  // DisplayXR inline-3D is ON BY DEFAULT on Android. This device exists to show
+  // glasses-free 3D; requiring an adb-written command-line file to reach that is
+  // not a default anyone can use, and it made the preview undemoable to every
+  // tester without developer tools (displayxr-browser#190).
+  //
+  // Set here, in the earliest callback, so the rest of startup — including the
+  // SurfaceControl suppression immediately below, which reads this same switch —
+  // sees a command line identical to one where the flag was passed by hand.
+  // --disable-inline-3d opts out; an explicit --enable-inline-3d stays a no-op.
+  if (!command_line.HasSwitch(switches::kProcessType) &&
+      !command_line.HasSwitch("disable-inline-3d") &&
+      !command_line.HasSwitch("enable-inline-3d")) {
+    // ForCurrentProcess() for the MUTABLE handle: `command_line` here is a
+    // const ref, so AppendSwitch on it does not compile. Same idiom as 0096's
+    // --disable-features append a few lines below.
+    base::CommandLine::ForCurrentProcess()->AppendSwitch("enable-inline-3d");
+    LOG(WARNING) << "[DisplayXR] inline-3D enabled by default "
+                    "(pass --disable-inline-3d to turn it off)";
+  }
+#endif  // BUILDFLAG(IS_ANDROID)
+
 #if BUILDFLAG(IS_ANDROID)
   // DisplayXR inline-3D on Android: the durable half of what has been a manual
   // /data/local/tmp/chrome-command-line flag on the device. Same shape and same
